WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.576
Integer safety in C++ is a practical engineering discipline.

2
00:00:03.576 --> 00:00:05.563
The roadmap begins with meaning.

3
00:00:05.563 --> 00:00:15.498
Know what each numeric value represents, what unit it uses, what range is valid, and what should happen when the value is outside that range.

4
00:00:15.498 --> 00:00:19.472
The type should support the domain rather than obscure it.

5
00:00:19.472 --> 00:00:21.061
Choose integer types deliberately.

6
00:00:21.061 --> 00:00:23.843
Use fixed-width types when representation matters.

7
00:00:23.843 --> 00:00:30.201
Use size_t where object and container sizes require it, but avoid casual mixing with signed values.

8
00:00:30.201 --> 00:00:35.764
Treat signedness and range as design decisions that affect portability, review, and failure behavior.

9
00:00:35.764 --> 00:00:38.944
Validate before converting, calculating, allocating, indexing, or copying.

10
00:00:38.944 --> 00:00:42.520
External values should be parsed and checked before use.

11
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:44.110
Arithmetic should have preconditions.

12
00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:51.660
Size calculations should make units visible and should confirm that the result is representable and acceptable for the application.

13
00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.839
Use APIs and code patterns that preserve intent.

14
00:00:54.839 --> 00:01:01.594
Clear contracts, domain-specific types, checked arithmetic helpers, explicit conversion functions, and consistent error handling make integer

15
00:01:01.594 --> 00:01:03.184
decisions easier to review.

16
00:01:03.184 --> 00:01:06.363
They also make invalid assumptions easier to test.

17
00:01:06.363 --> 00:01:09.542
Finally, make integer safety part of normal delivery.

18
00:01:09.542 --> 00:01:10.734
Add boundary tests.

19
00:01:10.734 --> 00:01:11.926
Enable useful warnings.

20
00:01:11.926 --> 00:01:15.105
Run sanitizer and diagnostic builds where they fit.

21
00:01:15.105 --> 00:01:19.874
Prioritize reviews in code that affects memory, bounds, permissions, and resource limits.

22
00:01:19.874 --> 00:01:25.040
Document remaining assumptions and revisit them as the codebase, compiler, and platform evolve.
